# Copy to apps/web/.env for local development.
#
# This file configures LOCAL development only. Production configuration lives in the Vercel
# project's environment variables and is never read from here — .vercelignore excludes every
# .env at every depth. Nothing needs switching between the two: the same code reads local
# values from this file and production values from the platform.
#
# In particular, do not point DATABASE_URL at the production database. `npm run db:reset` drops
# every table; it and `npm run db:seed` now refuse to run against a non-local host
# (apps/web/scripts/assert-local-db.mjs), but the first line of defence is that this file only
# ever names a local database.
#
# apps/web/.env — not .env.local — is the single local env file, because it is the one both
# Next.js and the Prisma CLI read. Every variable is validated on boot by apps/web/src/env.ts,
# so an invalid value fails fast with a message naming the offending variable.
#
# All values below are PLACEHOLDERS. Never commit real secrets.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database — required
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Matches the credentials in infra/docker-compose.dev.yml.
# In the production Docker stack the host is the service name, not localhost:
#   postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@postgres:5432/routely?schema=public
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://routely:routely@localhost:5432/routely?schema=public"

# Serverless only (see docs/DEPLOY-VERCEL.md). When DATABASE_URL points at a transaction
# pooler, migrations need a direct connection — a pooler cannot run the DDL and advisory locks
# Prisma migrations require. Leave unset when connecting straight to Postgres.
# DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=""

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication — required in production
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Auth.js session/token encryption key. At least 32 characters.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_SECRET="replace-with-a-32-character-random-string"

# Canonical public origin. This is Auth.js v5's replacement for NEXTAUTH_URL.
#
# It matters in production: `trustHost` is enabled so the app works behind the Nginx reverse
# proxy, and an explicit origin is what stops a forged Host header from redirecting the OAuth
# callback somewhere else. Production value: https://app.example.com
AUTH_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# Google OAuth 2.0 credentials from https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
#
# Authorised JavaScript origin:  http://localhost:3000
# Authorised redirect URI:       http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google
# In production, the same two values using your real origin.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID="replace-with-your-client-id.apps.googleusercontent.com"
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET="replace-with-your-client-secret"

# Development-only bypass that grants a placeholder session so the dashboard can be browsed
# without Google credentials. Forced off whenever NODE_ENV=production.
# Set this to "false" once the Google credentials above are real.
AUTH_DEV_BYPASS="true"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Application — optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Public origin of the dashboard, used for absolute links and install snippets.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL="http://localhost:3000"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tracking SDK — optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Absolute URL of the SDK bundle, as it appears in the install snippet customers paste into
# their sites. Public by necessity — every visitor's browser fetches it.
#
# Leave unset and the app serves the bundle from its own origin at NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL/sdk.js,
# which is what the snippet will show. Set it to move the bundle to a CDN in production:
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SDK_URL="https://cdn.example.com/sdk.js"
#
# Changing it does not break anything already installed — existing snippets keep loading from
# the URL they were generated with, so keep the old URL serving the bundle until customers
# update their sites.

# API origin baked into the SDK bundle at build time (used by the SDK to call back home).
# ROUTELY_API_BASE="http://localhost:3000"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Docker Compose — production stack only, see infra/
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# POSTGRES_USER="routely"
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD="replace-with-a-strong-password"
# POSTGRES_DB="routely"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Integrations — optional
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Google Sheets daily sync. Leave these unset and /integrations reports itself unavailable,
# naming what is missing; nothing else in the app is affected.

# Encrypts Google refresh tokens at rest. 32 random bytes, base64:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# A refresh token is the only value Routely stores that grants ongoing access to something
# outside Routely, so it is never stored in the clear. With no key, connecting is refused rather
# than downgraded.
# Deliberately NOT the same value as AUTH_SECRET: rotating AUTH_SECRET just signs everyone out,
# whereas rotating this makes every stored token undecryptable and forces every customer to
# reconnect. Those must not share one variable.
# TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=""

# Bearer token the scheduled sync endpoint (/api/cron/sheets-sync) requires:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# On Vercel, an environment variable of this exact name is sent automatically as
# `Authorization: Bearer <value>` on cron invocations — the name is Vercel's, not ours. Unset in
# production, the endpoint refuses every request rather than running unauthenticated.
# CRON_SECRET=""

# Google Picker, which is how a customer chooses one of their existing spreadsheets. Both are
# public by design: the Picker reads them from page script. The API key authorises nothing on its
# own — it identifies the Cloud project, while every file operation is authorised by the
# customer's own OAuth token. Restrict it to the Picker API and to your domains, and note that
# "your domains" must also include https://docs.google.com/* or the Picker fails to load.
# Without these two, only "create a new sheet" is offered, which still works.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_API_KEY=""
# The Cloud project NUMBER (all digits) — the part before the dash in GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID. Required
# by the Picker as its appId; without it a picked file is not associated with this app and the
# write that follows 404s.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_PROJECT_NUMBER=""

# The integration reuses GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID / GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET above — same Cloud project and
# the same consent screen. In the Google console it needs:
#   * Google Sheets API, Google Drive API and Google Picker API all enabled
#   * a second authorised REDIRECT URI (a different field from JavaScript origins):
#       http://localhost:3000/api/integrations/google/callback
#       https://your-app.example.com/api/integrations/google/callback
#   * authorised JavaScript origins for the app, for the Picker
#   * exactly these scopes on the consent screen's Data Access page, all non-sensitive, so no
#     Google verification review is required:
#       openid
#       .../auth/userinfo.email
#       .../auth/drive.file
# See docs/INTEGRATIONS.md.
